# Project working map

This is a FastAPI + Next.js unirepo. Run project commands from the Git root.
Read [the project profile](docs/internal/project-profile.md) for the current
[[ project_name ]] core, active capabilities, contracts, setup and authoritative
manifests. Examples, tests and docs must describe this boilerplate's current core.
Remove unrelated product material instead of archiving it in this repository.

## Architecture

- Backend: follow [backend/README.md](backend/README.md) and the linked guidance.
  Clean Architecture/DDD dependencies point inward. ORM models always live in
  `backend/src/common/database/models/`; shared domain models live in
  `backend/src/common/domain/models/`. Copy the appropriate full or thin module
  shape from the profile; do not invent missing layers.
- Use cases are dataclasses implementing `UseCase.execute()`. Domain repository
  ports have infrastructure adapters. Keep DomainError, presenters, router
  `add_api_route()`, explicit DI and request transaction/rollback conventions.
  Response classes own camelCase conversion; preserve RawJson exceptions.
- Before changing frontend, read [its architecture](docs/internal/architecture/frontend-architecture.md).
  Paths there are relative to `frontend/`. Keep `app -> features -> entities ->
  shared`, public slice facades and thin routes. Never reintroduce global DDD
  layers in frontend.
- Browser requests use same-origin `/api` BFF routes and shared HTTP helpers.
  Server-only requests may call the backend. Keep infrastructure credentials out
  of client code; verify both sides when HTTP contracts change.
- Before building/restyling UI, read [PRODUCT.md](PRODUCT.md) and [DESIGN.md](DESIGN.md).
  They govern strategy and visual design; live tokens are in frontend globals.css.
  Use impeccable for relevant design work. Preserve forms, i18n/themes, keyboard
  access and observable error/recovery states.
- Preserve active auth, tenancy, permissions, cache identity and session contracts
  from the profile. A skill example does not introduce or remove product rules.

## Development workflows

- Use define-change to establish scope, observable acceptance and verification.
  Reuse prior definition/authorization; a precise implementation request does not
  need another interview or approval.
- Use backend-change/frontend-change to build and refine their areas. Fullstack
  work composes both under one acceptance contract. Use schema-change for data,
  verify-change for tests/diagnosis, review-change for review without edits and
  validate-change for acceptance against the need.
- Functional work in either stack uses one OpenSpec change. Mechanical/docs work
  records concise acceptance/evidence in the existing task/PR/conversation.
- Build behavior through incremental red -> green -> refinement. Green checks,
  valid specs, complete task checkboxes and archive do not alone prove acceptance.
  Record evidence on the exact revision; renew affected results after changes.
- Record new module boundaries, contracts, persistence, security or core-flow
  decisions as Spanish [ADRs](docs/internal/adr/README.md).
  Store them in `docs/internal/adr/` using the index's required sections;
  external skill defaults do not create another ADR tree or replace this format.
- OpenSpec skills and generated opsx commands are upstream-managed: do not edit
  their content. The seven project workflows own orchestration and acceptance.
  Before invoking sync/archive for completed work, establish verified/validated
  closure through validate-change. Structural CLI output does not grant closure.
- A single integrator owns the combined diff and closure. Delegation is optional
  when authorized and useful for independent work. Writers need disjoint ownership
  or worktrees; shared contracts, lockfiles, models, wiring and CI have one owner.

## Commands and evidence

Use `just backend`, `just frontend`, `just docs` to discover recipes. Read
[verification.md](docs/internal/verification.md) for checks, services and impact.
`just check` is static; `just verify` adds backend tests and frontend tests/build.
API, browser, migration and docs checks remain explicit by impact. Quality/format/
lint repair recipes modify files; review and CI use checks without autofix.
Report commands, results and unverified behavior. Clean only owned resources;
worktrees do not isolate services. Do not use global prune as task cleanup.
Optional hooks: `lefthook install`; hooks complement CI and acceptance.
Use Git/rg and focused reads when optional MCP/LSP tools are absent.

## Skills and client configuration

Edit skills only in `.claude/skills/`. `.codex/skills/`, `.opencode/skills/` and
`.agents/skills/` are versioned subsets from `scripts/skill_inventory.json`.
The inventory identifies project-maintained skills. Codex discovers these only
through `.agents/skills/`; do not duplicate them in `.codex/skills/`.
Run `just sync-skills`, then `just agent-check`; commit sources and copies together.
The check must see drift before repair. Do not distribute private paths, secrets
or local trust state. CLAUDE.md imports this map; client integrations are optional.

## Template and style

Canonical repository only: the public Copier mirror is generated by
`scripts/build_template.py`, never edited manually. Branding/exclusions live in
`scripts/branding_tokens.json`; questionnaire in `template/copier.yml`. Validate
with `just template check` when these tools exist. Publishing is separate from
acceptance; preserve canonical redeploy guards. Generated projects do not maintain
or publish this template machinery.

Code/instruction files and commit messages are English. `docs/content/`,
`docs/internal/` and module READMEs are Spanish. Python uses snake_case, type hints
and async/await; TypeScript uses camelCase and functional components. Read runtime
versions and design tokens from their sources instead of copying them here.
